  "title": "Construction and trades",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Construction (a construção civil) and the trades employ many newcomers in Portugal, from large building sites to renovating flats. This deck has the site and the tools, the work itself, the short instructions you hear on site, safety, and talking to clients and the team. On site everyone says tu, short and loud; with a client in their own home, start with the third person.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in construction or a trade in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Servente de obras",
    "Pedreiro / Trolha",
    "Eletricista",
    "Canalizador/a",
    "Pintor/a",
    "Carpinteiro/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Instructions on site are short: «Anda cá!», «Passa-me o martelo», «Para!» If you didn't understand, say so: «Não percebi, podes repetir?» Never guess with machines.",
    "Wear your helmet, safety boots and vest from the first minute. On scaffolding and roofs you need protection against falls; ask for it if it's missing.",
    "Many construction workers are hired through subcontractors or temporary agencies. Ask who your employer is, get a contract and payslips, and check that you are covered by work accident insurance (seguro de acidentes de trabalho). General orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
